+# 03: Reservations expire on their own
+
+**What to build:** A reservation nobody acts on expires 20 minutes after it was created, giving its units back. There is no background job: expiry takes effect purely because time, read from the injected clock, has passed, and every read (lookup, stock figures, retries) sees it. The lifetime can be overridden when constructing the service. See the spec (`spec.md` in this feature directory).
+
+**Blocked by:** 02 (Safe retries with an idempotency key)
+
+**Status:** resolved
+
+- [x] Before `expires_at`, the reservation is `ACTIVE` and its units count as reserved
+- [x] At exactly `expires_at`, and after, `get_reservation` reports `EXPIRED`
+- [x] At exactly `expires_at`, and after, its units no longer count as reserved and are available again (to `available`, new reservations and `place_order`)
+- [x] Expiry takes effect without calling any sweep or cleanup method
+- [x] The service constructor accepts an optional `reservation_ttl` keyword (a time duration) defaulting to 20 minutes; a custom value changes `expires_at` and when expiry happens
+- [x] Constructing the service with only repository, ledger and clock still works
+- [x] A repeated `reserve` with the same key and request after expiry returns the reservation as `EXPIRED` and reserves nothing new
+- [x] A `reserve` reusing the key with a different request after expiry still raises `IdempotencyConflict`
+- [x] All times come from the injected clock
+
