+# Stock reservations
+
+Status: resolved
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Checkout has no way to claim stock for a cart while the customer pays. Today stock is only promised once an order is placed, so between "customer clicks checkout" and "payment succeeds" another cart can take the same units, and the customer who just paid finds the goods gone. The cart service also retries calls on network failures, and with nothing but `place_order` available a retry can claim stock twice. Abandoned checkouts must not tie up stock forever, and there is no background job to clean them up.
+
+## Solution
+
+Introduce **Reservations**: a time-limited claim on stock in a single warehouse, taken for a cart before payment. The cart service reserves stock with an idempotency key; the payment service then confirms the reservation into an Order, or checkout releases it. A reservation nobody confirms expires on its own 20 minutes after creation — expiry is derived from the injected clock whenever state is read, so no sweep is needed. Reserved units are subtracted from available stock, so other carts and regular orders cannot take them.
+
+Reservations take whatever is available (see ADR-0001): if a cart asks for 6 and only 5 are available, it gets a reservation for 5, and the cart shows the shortfall by comparing what was reserved with what it asked for. Orders placed directly remain all-or-nothing.
+
+## User Stories
+
+### Reserving
+
+1. As the cart service, I want to reserve stock for a cart in one warehouse, so that the units are not sold to anyone else while the customer pays.
+2. As the cart service, I want a single reservation to cover several SKUs at once, so that a whole cart is claimed in one call.
+3. As the cart service, I want each line to reserve as many units as are available up to what I asked for, so that the customer can still buy what is in stock when one line is short.
+4. As the cart service, I want lines with nothing available left out of the reservation's items entirely, so that I never see zero-quantity lines.
+5. As the cart service, I want the reservation's items to show exactly the units reserved per SKU, so that I can show the customer any shortfall by comparing with my request.
+6. As the cart service, I want reserve to fail with an insufficient-stock error when no line can reserve anything, so that I don't get an empty reservation.
+7. As the cart service, I want a failed reserve to leave no reservation behind, so that nothing is claimed when the request fails.
+8. As the cart service, I want a reserve naming an unknown warehouse or SKU to be rejected exactly like an order would be, so that validation is consistent across checkout.
+9. As the cart service, I want a reserve with a non-positive or non-integer quantity to be rejected, so that malformed carts are caught.
+10. As the cart service, I want a rejected request to reserve nothing, not even for its valid lines, so that validation failures are all-or-nothing.
+11. As the cart service, I want a reserve with no items to be rejected, so that empty carts don't create reservations.
+12. As the cart service, I want a reserve with an empty idempotency key to be rejected, so that every reservation is retry-safe.
+13. As the cart service, I want each reservation to have a unique identifier, so that I can confirm, release or look it up later.
+14. As the cart service, I want each new reservation to start out active, so that its units are immediately protected.
+15. As the cart service, I want each reservation to state when it expires, so that I can show the customer a countdown.
+
+### Retrying safely (idempotency)
+
+16. As the cart service, I want to repeat a reserve with the same idempotency key and the same request and get the original reservation back, so that a network retry never claims stock twice.
+17. As the cart service, I want a repeated reserve to compare against what I originally asked for, not what was reserved, so that a partially filled reservation still counts as a match on retry.
+18. As the cart service, I want the order of items in my request not to matter when matching a retry, so that equivalent requests are treated as equal.
+19. As the cart service, I want a repeated reserve to return the reservation as it is now, even if it has since been confirmed, released or has expired, so that a key always refers to exactly one reservation.
+20. As the cart service, I want a repeated reserve not to move the expiry time, so that retries cannot keep stock claimed indefinitely.
+21. As the cart service, I want reusing a key with a different warehouse or different items to fail with an idempotency conflict, so that a key bug on my side is caught instead of silently returning the wrong reservation.
+22. As the cart service, I want that conflict to apply even when the original reservation was released or expired, so that keys are never recycled.
+23. As the cart service, I want a key whose reserve failed to stay unused, so that I can retry the same key once stock arrives.
+24. As the cart service, I want idempotency keys to be unique across all warehouses, so that I don't have to namespace them myself.
+
+### Expiry
+
+25. As the operations team, I want a reservation that nobody confirms to expire 20 minutes after it was created, so that abandoned checkouts give their units back.
+26. As the operations team, I want expiry to take effect without anyone running a cleanup job, so that stock is never stuck because a job didn't run.
+27. As the operations team, I want a reservation to count as expired from the moment its expiry time is reached, so that the boundary is unambiguous.
+28. As the operations team, I want expired reservations to stop counting as reserved immediately, so that their units can be sold again right away.
+29. As the cart service, I want looking up a reservation past its expiry to report it as expired, so that I can tell the customer their checkout timed out.
+30. As the payment service, I want confirmed reservations never to expire, so that a paid order is never undone by the clock.
+31. As the cart service, I want released reservations to stay released after their expiry time, so that their history stays accurate.
+32. As whoever builds the service, I want the reservation lifetime to default to 20 minutes and be overridable when constructing the service, so that other deployments or simulations can tune it without code changes.
+33. As a developer, I want all reservation times to come from the injected clock, so that expiry is reproducible in tests and simulations.
+
+### Confirming
+
+34. As the payment service, I want to confirm an active reservation into an order, so that a paid cart becomes a fulfilment commitment.
+35. As the payment service, I want the order to contain exactly the units the reservation held, so that a partial reservation turns into a partial order.
+36. As the payment service, I want the order to be a normal placed order with the next order number and the confirmation time as its placement time, so that it ships and cancels like any other order.
+37. As the payment service, I want the reservation to record which order it became and be marked confirmed, so that the two can be traced to each other.
+38. As the operations team, I want confirming not to change available stock, so that the reserved units simply become allocated units.
+39. As the payment service, I want confirm to use the reserved units without re-checking availability, so that a customer who paid for reserved stock always gets an order.
+40. As the payment service, I want repeating a confirm to return the same order without creating another, so that my retries are safe.
+41. As the payment service, I want a repeated confirm to work even after the 20 minutes have passed, so that a slow retry doesn't fail.
+42. As the payment service, I want a repeated confirm to return the order as it is now, even if it was since cancelled or shipped, without error, so that retries never fail on later state changes.
+43. As the payment service, I want confirming an expired reservation to fail with a reservation-expired error, including one that expired only just now, so that I can refund instead of shipping stock that is no longer claimed.
+44. As the payment service, I want confirming a released reservation to fail with an invalid-state error, so that a cancelled checkout cannot be revived.
+
+### Releasing
+
+45. As the cart service, I want to release an active reservation, so that a customer who abandons checkout gives the units back immediately rather than after 20 minutes.
+46. As the cart service, I want a released reservation's units to become available at once, so that other customers can buy them.
+47. As the cart service, I want releasing an already released reservation to return it unchanged without error, so that my retries are safe.
+48. As the cart service, I want releasing an expired reservation to return it unchanged, still expired, without error, so that a late cleanup call doesn't fail.
+49. As the cart service, I want releasing a confirmed reservation to fail with an invalid-state error, so that stock belonging to a paid order cannot be given back through checkout.
+
+### Looking up
+
+50. As the cart service, I want to look up a reservation by its identifier and see its warehouse, reserved items, status, expiry time and order identifier, so that I can render checkout state.
+51. As the cart service, I want looking up an unknown reservation to fail with a reservation-not-found error, so that I can tell a bad identifier apart from other failures.
+
+### Stock figures
+
+52. As the operations team, I want available stock to be on-hand minus allocated minus reserved, never below zero, so that it reflects what can still be promised.
+53. As the operations team, I want to query how many units of a SKU are reserved in a warehouse, so that I can see how much stock is claimed by checkouts in progress.
+54. As the operations team, I want reserved to count only active, unexpired reservations, so that released, expired and confirmed reservations don't inflate it.
+55. As the operations team, I want allocated to keep counting only placed orders, so that the existing meaning of allocated doesn't change.
+56. As the operations team, I want a reservation in one warehouse not to affect stock figures in any other warehouse, so that warehouses remain independent.
+57. As the operations team, I want a reservation for one SKU not to affect other SKUs, so that figures are tracked per SKU.
+58. As a customer placing an order directly, I want placing an order to respect reserved units, so that I'm not promised stock already claimed at checkout.
+59. As a customer placing an order directly, I want placing an order to stay all-or-nothing, so that direct orders behave exactly as before.
+60. As a back-office user, I want the stock report to show reserved units per SKU alongside on-hand, allocated and available, so that I can see why available is lower than expected.
+61. As a back-office user, I want existing stock report consumers to keep working, so that adding the reserved column doesn't break them.
+
+### Orders after confirmation
+
+62. As the warehouse, I want an order created from a reservation to ship like any other order, so that fulfilment needs no special handling.
+63. As customer service, I want to be able to cancel an order created from a reservation like any other order, freeing its allocation, so that refunds work normally.
+64. As customer service, I want cancelling such an order to leave the reservation marked confirmed, so that the reservation's history stays accurate.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+- **Vocabulary** follows the glossary: Reservation, Reserved, Confirm, Release, Expire, Idempotency key. "Hold", "lapse" and "cancel" (for reservations) are not used in code or docs.
+- **Models**: a new `ReservationStatus` enum with members `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED`, `EXPIRED`, and a mutable `Reservation` dataclass, like `Order`, with exactly these public fields: `reservation_id: str`, `warehouse: str`, `items: dict[str, int]` (units reserved per SKU, with zero lines omitted), `status: ReservationStatus`, `expires_at: datetime`, `order_id: str | None`. The requested quantities and the idempotency key are kept by the repository/service and are not part of the public shape of `Reservation` (decided: the cart compares `items` with its own request).
+- **Errors**: four new subclasses of `InventoryError`: `ReservationNotFound` (unknown identifier), `ReservationExpired` (confirm after expiry), `InvalidReservationState` (confirm on released, release on confirmed), `IdempotencyConflict` (same key, different request). Existing errors are reused: `UnknownWarehouse`, `UnknownSku` and `InvalidQuantity` for validation, `InvalidOrder` for empty items or an empty or blank key, and `InsufficientStock` when no line can reserve anything (it names the first requested SKU, with available 0).
+- **Service interface**: new methods on `InventoryService`:
+  - `reserve(warehouse, items, idempotency_key) -> Reservation`
+  - `confirm_reservation(reservation_id) -> Order`
+  - `release_reservation(reservation_id) -> Reservation`
+  - `get_reservation(reservation_id) -> Reservation`
+  - `reserved(sku, warehouse) -> int`
+- **Constructor**: `InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` keeps working unchanged. A new optional keyword argument, `reservation_ttl` (a timedelta), defaults to 20 minutes. `expires_at` is the clock time at creation plus the TTL.
+- **Reserve algorithm**:
+  1. Validate the request exactly like `place_order`: warehouse, SKUs, quantities, non-empty items. Also reject an empty or blank key. Validation errors take precedence over idempotency conflicts.
+  2. If the key is already bound, compare the warehouse and requested quantities per SKU (order-insensitive). If they match, return the existing reservation in its current state without touching `expires_at`. If they don't, raise `IdempotencyConflict`.
+  3. Otherwise compute `min(requested, available)` per line and drop lines with zero. If nothing remains, raise `InsufficientStock` and store nothing, leaving the key unbound. If something remains, create an `ACTIVE` reservation with the next reservation identifier, store it together with its requested quantities, and bind the key.
+- **Expiry is computed when state is read, not by a sweep**. A reservation is effectively `EXPIRED` when its stored status is `ACTIVE` and `clock.now() >= expires_at`. Every read path (`get_reservation`, `reserved`/`available`, confirm, release, idempotent replay) must see the effective status. Whether the effective status is also written back to storage is up to the implementer, but behaviour must be the same either way. Only `ACTIVE` reservations ever become `EXPIRED`.
+- **State transitions**:
+
+  | Current (effective) status | confirm                                   | release                     |
+  | -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------- |
+  | ACTIVE                     | create PLACED order → CONFIRMED, set `order_id` | → RELEASED            |
+  | CONFIRMED                  | return the existing order as it is now (no new order) | `InvalidReservationState` |
+  | RELEASED                   | `InvalidReservationState`                 | no-op, return unchanged     |
+  | EXPIRED                    | `ReservationExpired`                      | no-op, return unchanged     |
+
+- **Confirm** builds the order from the reservation's `items` (the reserved units, not the requested ones). It uses the normal order identifier sequence, status `PLACED` and `placed_at` set to now. It does not re-check availability, because the reserved units move straight from reserved to allocated and `available` doesn't change.
+- **Stock queries**: `reserved(sku, warehouse)` sums the units of that SKU in active, unexpired reservations in that warehouse. `available` becomes `max(0, on_hand − allocated − reserved)`. `allocated` keeps counting only `PLACED` orders. `place_order` keeps checking `available`, so it respects reservations automatically, and stays all-or-nothing.
+- **Repository**: gains reservation storage (save/get by identifier, raising `ReservationNotFound` for unknown ones, plus listing by warehouse), a reservation identifier sequence in the style of the order sequence, and a lookup from idempotency key to reservation. The repository stays swappable for a database-backed one.
+- **Stock report**: `StockRow` gains a `reserved` field with a default of 0, added **after** the existing fields so existing positional construction keeps working. `stock_report` fills it from `reserved`.
+- **Package exports**: `Reservation` and `ReservationStatus` are exported from the package root next to the other models.
+- **README**: the Concepts section gains Reserved and the reservation lifecycle, and the definition of Available is updated to match the glossary.
+- **ADR-0001** (partial reservations) applies: reserve fills lines partially while `place_order` stays all-or-nothing. Don't "fix" this.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **Good tests check behaviour you can see from outside**: they call public service methods and look at returned objects, stock figures and raised error types. They do not reach into repository internals, how requested quantities are stored, or how expiry is evaluated. A test should survive a rewrite of the storage.
+- **One seam**: the public `InventoryService` API, built from the existing `conftest` fixtures (`repo`, `ledger`, `clock`, `service`). Time moves only through `FixedClock.advance`/`set`. No sweep or private helper is called.
+- **Secondary check**: one test through `stock_report` for the new `reserved` column. It's an existing public read model with its own test file.
+- **What to cover**: partial fills and dropped lines; the all-lines-short failure leaving no trace and the key reusable; validation rejecting the whole request; idempotent replay (same id, no extra stock, `expires_at` unchanged, works after confirm/release/expiry); idempotency conflict (different warehouse, different items, after release/expiry); expiry exactly at `expires_at` and after; `available` and `reserved` before and after each transition; confirm producing an order of the reserved units with no change to `available`; repeated confirm after the TTL and after the order was cancelled or shipped; every cell of the transition table; `ReservationNotFound`; `place_order` blocked by reserved units; warehouse and SKU isolation; a custom `reservation_ttl`; the existing constructor still working.
+- **Prior art**: `tests/test_orders.py` (a `stocked` fixture, one behaviour per test, error types asserted with `pytest.raises`, clock advanced for time-dependent assertions) and `tests/test_reports.py` (whole-report equality). New tests go in a dedicated reservations test module. The existing tests must keep passing unchanged.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Extending or refreshing a reservation's expiry.
+- Editing a reservation's items after creation.
+- Partially confirming a reservation (confirming some lines or fewer units).
+- A background sweep or scheduled expiry job.
+- Changing `place_order` to fill partially, or giving it an idempotency key.
+- Idempotency keys for confirm or release (repeated calls are already safe by the state rules above).
+- Guarding write-offs (`adjust`) against dropping on-hand below allocated + reserved. Existing behaviour is unchanged.
+- Expiring or garbage-collecting idempotency keys. A key stays bound permanently.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- If a write-off has shrunk on-hand below what is promised, confirm may either succeed or raise `InsufficientStock`. The business owner is indifferent. The intended implementation doesn't re-check, so it succeeds.
+- The interface above is what an external (hidden) test suite calls. Method names, argument order, model field names and enum member names must match exactly.
+- Glossary: `GLOSSARY.md`. Decision record: `docs/adr/0001-partial-reservations.md`.
